            <ArticleTitle>Self-assembled carbohydrate hydrogels for prolonged pain management.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>990-1004</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3109/10837450.2011.609992</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="CONTEXT" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Nanoparticulate systems are new tools that promise a revolution in the field of drug delivery. Among their numerous benefits, polyelectrolyte complexes (PECs) have shown to provide a barrier to drug release.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">In this study, PECs, in the form of self-assembled polymeric nanogels, have been studied as potential drug carriers of the freely soluble drug tramadol HCL trying to achieve a prolonged percutaneous permeation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODOLOGY" NlmCategory="METHODS">The hydrogels were subjected to swelling, rheology, release and permeation studies and were characterized by FTIR spectroscopy and scanning electron microscopy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">P2 hydrogel composed of chitosan-carrageenan (1-1) PEC attained the most compromised rheological shear-thinning thixotropic behavior, good bioadhesive properties, the most retarded release and permeation with an f2 value &lt;50 compared to chitosan hydrogel, altogether with non-irritancy to the skin. SEM photographs showed that P2 has spherical nanosized particles structure.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">This approach can provide us promising results for an around-the-clock analgesia with better safety and tolerability profile.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
